#[[no_drop_all]]
/* pointer.crl: parking lot, not code.

 * pointer/arithmetic static analysis would live here. nothing implemented
 * yet; this checklist is here so the ideas don't evaporate.
 * everything below is C-flavored on purpose; that's the interop surface,
 * and C is where these bite. no codes yet: the SYN-/SEM- numbers in the
 * old notes were invented, whatever gets implemented picks real ones. */

// ── pointer arithmetic ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// void* / function-pointer math: void* has no element size and `fn + 1`
// walks through code. make people cast to cchar*/u8* first.
//   bad:  void* next = data + 4;
//   good: cchar* next = (cchar*)data + 4;
//
// `p + q`: two addresses added together. meaningless; pointer + integer
// offset is the only sensible form.
//
// `p * 2`, `p / 2`, `p % 2`, `p << 1`, `p & ~3`: addresses are not
// numbers. do the bit math on a usize and cast back.
//
// `-p`: negating an address. negate the offset instead.
//
// static out-of-bounds: `buf[64]` and `buf + 128`; the bound is known at
// compile time, so flag it. one-past-the-end is fine, past that isn't.
//
// subtracting pointers from unrelated blocks (`&a[2] - &b[5]`) is UB.
// "same block" needs alias analysis; the obvious cases (two different
// locals/globals) are catchable without it.
//
// null arithmetic: `p = null; p += 4`; dataflow, not types. only catch it
// when the null literal is right there.
//
// double scaling: `items + 2 * @sizeof(int)` scales twice; that's +80,
// not +20. warn on @sizeof(pointee) inside pointer math.
//
// dead roots: after free() or a local's scope ends, `buf + 2` is garbage
// even though nothing is dereferenced. lifetime tracking is probably the
// runtime/valgrind side's job, not sema's.
//
// unaligned punning: stepping a char buffer by 3 and casting to u32*.
// can't express alignment in the type system; lint territory. memcpy is
// the fixed answer.
//
// decayed array params: `f(int arr[4])` in a C header is just int*; the
// 4 is gone. nothing left to bound after decay; the caller must pass a
// count.
//
// struct field wandering: `p = &u.code[0]; (int*)(p + 8)` hops fields by
// hand. flag the manual stride, point at `&u.status` instead.
//
// writing through a string literal: literals live in rodata;
// `*(s+1) = 'a'` is a segfault with homework. coral str is an immutable
// slice view anyway; this one is for the cchar* interop path.
//
// stride mismatch: stepping a struct* by a raw byte count (+10) instead
// of whole elements. warn when the step isn't a multiple of the element
// size.
//
// relational compares across allocations: `<` between pointers is only
// defined within one block. same alias problem as subtraction; warn on
// the obvious cases.
//
// signed/unsigned offset wrap: `u32 off = len - 10` with len < 10 becomes
// ~4 billion and `buf + off` is long gone. flag offsets built by
// subtracting an unsigned length.
//
// volatile drop: `volatile u32* r; u32* n = r + 1` loses the qualifier.
// same shape as the pointer-const rules: assignments may add qualifiers,
// not remove them.

// ── pointer ops that are flat out illegal ───────────────────────────────
//
// literal into a pointer: only 0/null is writable without a cast;
// `int* p = 0x7FFF0012` needs the explicit cast. the int→pointer assign
// path already reports TC-0009 (non-fatal under --allow-int-ptr); this
// is the same idea generalized to nonzero literals.
//
// returning a stack address: `return &local`, dangling before the caller
// finishes reading. real fix needs escape analysis; a shallow version can
// catch returning a local that was declared right there.
//
// double free: the same pointer into free() twice. heap metadata
// corruption. a within-one-function tracker (free, then use/free of that
// same local) catches the easy cases.
//
// wild uninitialized pointer: locals aren't zeroed; `*out = 5` on a
// never-assigned `int* out` writes into stack junk. warn on deref of a
// local pointer that was never assigned, null or otherwise.
//
// incompatible pointer assignment without a cast: `User* u = car`.
// strict aliasing says no; C just warns. we can require the explicit cast.

// ── non-pointer arithmetic traps ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// int division into a float: `double r = 3 / 4` is 0.0; the division
// happens before the assignment ever sees a float. coral's
// no-implicit-conversion rule makes this unrepresentable here; keep it in
// mind for the C interop path.
//
// shift >= width: `1 << 32` is UB; on x86 the hardware masks the amount
// to 0 so it "works". warn on literal shift amounts that don't fit.
//
// `%` on floats: rejected by the C grammar; fmod is the escape hatch.
//
// signed overflow: `val + 1` at INT_MAX is UB and optimizers lean on it.
// catch literals at compile time; runtime checks are out of scope for now.
//
// float equality after math: `0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3`. a lint, not a type error.
//
// assignment in condition: `if (x = 5)`; C assigns, condition is always
// true. assignments are expressions here too, so this may well type-check;
// it deserves a dedicated diagnostic.
//
// unsigned underflow loop: `for (u32 i = 10; i >= 0; i--)` never stops.
// warn when an unsigned loop variable is compared `>= 0`.
